# Staging verification fixtures are checked out byte-for-byte on every platform.
#
# `manifest.json` records each fixture's size and SHA-256 so an operator can
# confirm the file they are about to upload is the one the answer key describes.
# That only works if the bytes on disk are the same everywhere. Without this,
# Git's autocrlf on Windows rewrites every LF to CRLF at checkout and the file
# gains one byte per line -- 150 for `plain-conversation.json` -- so the digest
# check fails on a clone that is in fact correct.
#
# Deliberately scoped to these files rather than the repository. A repo-wide
# `text=auto` would rewrite every contributor's working tree, which is a much
# larger decision than this one and is not needed to make a digest meaningful.
docs/ops/external-conversation-continuation-staging-verification-records/fixtures/*.json text eol=lf

# AI Review evaluation records are bound to their approvals by digest.
#
# The blind sheet, the record a person signs, the answer key and the run
# journal are all fingerprinted, and the sheet is additionally rebuilt and
# compared byte for byte. Git's autocrlf on Windows rewrites LF to CRLF at
# checkout, so evidence that verifies on the machine that made it -- and in
# CI, which is Linux -- would fail on a fresh Windows clone of the same commit.
#
# That asymmetry is the worst shape this could take: the operator whose
# approval it is sees a digest mismatch that nobody else can reproduce, and
# the honest reading of a digest mismatch is that the evidence was tampered
# with. Pinning the whole directory rather than four globs, because anything
# written beside these files is evidence too.
docs/ops/ai-review-evaluation-records/** text eol=lf
docs/ops/ai-review-evaluation-set/*.json text eol=lf
